+*   **Measured Performance:** VS-Splat (using SFR) achieves about a 1.3× speedup over LaRa on the GObjaverse dataset [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Eliminates the semantic gap of implicit mapping, enhances generalization via commonality learning, and handles unposed multi-view inputs robustly [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires careful selection of voxels in voxel-selective variants [S2, unofficial]. Lidar-based fusion requires handling occlusion [S3].
+*   **When to Use:** When generalizing to unseen scenes is required, and semantic information from reference images can improve rendering quality [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** When computational resources are extremely limited and pre-extracted features are not available [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method originates from GARF (Geometry-Aware Generalized Neural Radiance Field) [S1, unofficial], which introduced the concept of conditioning NeRF on deep convolution features to improve generalization. Variants include VS-Splat [S2, unofficial], LidaRF [S3], and FWD [S4].
+The concept of Encoder and Feature Fusion was introduced in the context of Geometry-Aware Generalized Neural Radiance Fields (GARF) [S1, unofficial]. It was developed to address the limitations of NeRF in generalizing to new scenes by incorporating deep convolutional features [S1, unofficial]. The method builds on the idea that semantic information from 2D images can guide 3D reconstruction [S1, unofficial]. Variants such as VS-Splat [S2, unofficial], LidaRF [S3], FWD [S4], and DP-NeRF [S5] have extended this concept to specific domains like sparse-view reconstruction, Lidar integration, forward warping, and deblurring.
 
